<h1>Hello from a .hd file</h1>
<p>This is a body-only HTML fragment — no <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, or <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> wrappers. Just semantic content.</p>

<h2>Why this matters</h2>
<p>Markdown can't easily express things like the table below — merged cells, multi-line content, or inline formatting inside cells. HTML can. The <code>.hd</code> extension signals "documentation HTML fragment" rather than a full HTML page.</p>

<h2>A table that markdown can't cleanly render</h2>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>Markdown</th>
      <th>.hd</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Basic tables</td>
      <td>Yes</td>
      <td>Yes</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Merged cells (colspan/rowspan)</td>
      <td>No</td>
      <td><strong>Yes</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Multi-paragraph cells</td>
      <td>No</td>
      <td><strong>Yes</strong> — like this<br><br>Second paragraph in the same cell.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td colspan="2" style="text-align:center"><em>Merged across two columns →</em></td>
      <td>Works fine</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>A list</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Headings, paragraphs, lists, links — all standard HTML</li>
  <li>No <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags allowed in the document</li>
  <li>Assets stored alongside the file in a sibling folder (TBD convention)</li>
</ul>

<p><a href="https://claude.com/blog/using-claude-code-the-unreasonable-effectiveness-of-html">Reference: Claude blog post on HTML over Markdown</a></p>
